.TH QANDROID 1 "2026-11-27" "qandroid 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qandroid \- run arm64 Android APKs on x86_64 Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qandroid
\fIcommand\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qandroid
runs arm64 Android applications on an x86_64 Linux host. It combines
\fBqemu-aarch64-user\fR for instruction translation, the host kernel for the
architecture-independent half of the Android ABI (\fBbinderfs\fR, \fBmemfd\fR,
\fBfutex\fR, namespaces), and an unmodified arm64 Android userspace on top.
.PP
The guest is a real Android userspace: bionic, ART and SurfaceFlinger are the
platform's own code. Nothing about Android is reimplemented; only the
instruction set is translated.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B doctor
Check host prerequisites: page size, qemu, binfmt registration, kernel binderfs,
memfd, ext4 tooling and whether a rootfs exists.
.TP
.B setup
Fetch an arm64 system and vendor image, then build a rootfs from them. Equivalent
to \fBimage fetch\fR followed by \fBrootfs build\fR.
.TP
.B image list
List the newest arm64 images on the Waydroid mirror, with sizes and checksums.
.TP
.B image fetch
Download an image (system zip plus the separate vendor partition) and unpack
\fIsystem.img\fR and \fIvendor.img\fR beside it.
.TP
.B rootfs build
Probe the image layout (system-as-root or a bare \fI/system\fR partition), extract
it with \fBdebugfs\fR (no loop devices, no root), and report whether init, the
dynamic linker and the app_process binary are where the guest will look.
.TP
.B rootfs shell
Enter the guest shell, or run a single command in it.
.TP
.B apk info
Inspect an APK offline: package id, version, SDK levels, permissions, ABIs and
native libraries.
.TP
.B apk install
Install an APK into a running guest through the platform's own
\fBpm install\fR.
.TP
.B apk start
Launch an installed package.
.TP
.B boot
Start the guest userspace under QEMU inside its own PID namespace, with binderfs
devices, flattened APEXes bound into \fI/apex\fR and a generated
\fI/linkerconfig/ld.config.txt\fR.
.TP
.B display
Open the HWC2 display client: the guest's SurfaceFlinger frames in an SDL2
window, with a uinput virtual touchscreen for input.
.TP
.B logs
Show the guest boot log (\fI\-f\fR follows it).
.TP
.B stop
Stop a running guest.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/qandroid/
Per-user state: \fIimages/\fR, \fIrootfs/\fR, \fIlogs/\fR and \fIconfig.json\fR.
Set \fBQANDROID_HOME\fR to relocate it.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/qandroid/
Installed payload: the CLI, tools, scripts, qemu patches, HWC module sources and
the display client.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QANDROID_HOME
State directory (default \fI~/.local/share/qandroid\fR).
.TP
.B QANDROID_QEMU
qemu interpreter to use (default \fBqemu-aarch64-static\fR).
.TP
.B QANDROID_DISPLAY
Guest display geometry as \fIWxH@Hz\fR for the composer module.
.TP
.B QANDROID_HWC_SOCK
Composer socket path inside the guest.
.TP
.B QANDROID_STRACE
Set to \fB1\fR to pass \fBQEMU_STRACE\fR through to the guest's syscall log.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
qandroid doctor
qandroid setup
sudo qandroid boot
sudo qandroid apk install app.apk
qandroid display
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
32-bit (\fIarmeabi-v7a\fR) APKs are not supported, and the guest has no GPU: the
composer composites on the CPU. Binder IPC needs a qemu built with the patches in
\fI/usr/lib/qandroid/patches\fR; the distro build returns \fBENOTTY\fR for binder
ioctls. See \fI/usr/share/doc/qandroid/docs/BRINGUP.md\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qemu-aarch64 (1),
.BR apk (1)
.PP
Full documentation: \fI/usr/share/doc/qandroid/\fR.
